From 8d27fc94f2e6499d8e5bfd66b5167c5167c8cf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: G31_KbaseShim3 <shim@gpu31>
Date: Thu, 19 Nov 2026 00:00:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] gpu31-shim: optional render node backed by kbase UK 10.6 (H2)

A new, self-contained DRM driver that exposes ONE render node
(/dev/dri/renderD128) whose file operations forward to kbase's own
(kbase_open/create_context, release/destroy, read/poll/ioctl/mmap).
Userspace on the node speaks the kbase UK 10.6 in-buffer ABI
(VERSION_CHECK, SET_FLAGS, GPU_PROPS, MEM_ALLOC, JOB_SUBMIT stride-64,
SAME_VA mmap, event read/poll) exactly as on /dev/mali0, so the existing
kbase winsys (code/userspace/gpu/kbase-winsys) and the Mesa kbase backend
(patches/gpu-mesa-kbase + the render-node adapt diff) run unmodified
apart from the node path they open.

Why a driver, not a flag on exynos: kbase owns its GPU context state per
open file (filp->private_data = kctx, created in kbase_open, destroyed in
kbase_release; ioctl/read/poll/mmap all take the kctx from the file).
A DRM open on the exynos driver instead installs a drm_file and routes
ioctls through the DRM dispatcher, which copies fixed-size structs and
rejects unknown ioctl numbers before any driver code runs.  The kbase
ioctl path needs the opposite: one ioctl number carrying a variable-size
buffer whose first u32 is the function id, dispatched in-buffer by
kbase_dispatch().  Bolting that onto the exynos drm_driver would mean
replacing its open/ioctl/mmap dispatch for every client, including the
display path.  A separate driver keeps the display path byte-identical
and makes the shim removable with one Kconfig off-switch.

What this is and is not:
- IS: a render node (DRM_MINOR_RENDER only; no primary/control minors, no
  CRTC/encoder/connector, no modeset, no fbdev claim).  PRIME and dumb
  ioctls are absent on this node; GPU/display buffer sharing stays with
  the exynos card0 path (G31_DmaBuf import-by-copy), unchanged.
- IS: optional (own Kconfig symbol DRM_KBASE_SHIM, default n; only builds
  when the integrator selects it; never selected by the M1 fragment).
- IS: non-invasive (one new directory + Kconfig/Makefile lines; zero
  hunks in existing files; exynos driver, DRM core, kbase driver, DT and
  defconfig untouched; compiles out to nothing when disabled).
- IS NOT: a second GPU driver.  It programs no registers, takes no
  clocks/power/IRQ, owns no display pipe.  All GPU state lives in the
  kbase driver it borrows contexts from.
- IS NOT: a panfrost UAPI node (H1).  If the integrator later lands a
  panfrost GEM/job-slot driver with DRM_IOCTL_PANFROST_* on the render
  node, this shim is superseded and should be turned off (they must never
  both bind the render minor).

Design (mirrors the Mode B render-node patch shape, G31_Kms):
- Open creates BOTH states: the DRM core installs the drm_file
  (drm_open_helper: GEM open + PRIME init + master handling), then the
  driver's .open hook creates the kbase context exactly as kbase_open()
  does (kbase_find_device + kbase_create_context, is_compat_task-aware)
  and stores it in priv->driver_priv.  One open = one kctx, one close =
  one destroy -- same 1:1 as /dev/mali0.
- Every kbase file operation swaps filp->private_data to the kctx for
  the call and restores the drm_file after: ioctl (UK dispatch on the
  in-buffer id, KMS numbers refused first), read (event stream), poll
  (event wait), mmap (SAME_VA cookies + tracking page).  kbase takes its
  own per-context locks; the swap is per-filp and introduces no new
  locking.  Release destroys the kctx first, then runs the stock
  drm_release() body for the drm_file (preclose already ran inside it).
- struct drm_device exists for node plumbing only: the render minor id
  (128..383 carve-out, first free = 128 -> renderD128 via the
  already-wired drm_sysfs renderD%d naming), GEM init (DRIVER_GEM so
  drm_gem_open runs; harmless, no GEM objects are created by this
  driver), and the ioctl table the dispatcher needs for VERSION.
- drm_driver hooks: .open creates the kctx; .preclose/.postclose are
  no-ops for kbase state (it dies in release); .fops->open = drm_open
  (installs the drm_file), .mmap/.read/.poll/.ioctl wrappers forward
  with the kctx installed, .release destroys the kctx then calls
  drm_release.  No DRM core or kbase function is patched or copied.
- ioctl routing: the driver's table holds exactly ONE entry,
  DRM_IOCTL_VERSION (DRM_UNLOCKED, always allowed).  The fops wrapper
  serves VERSION through the DRM dispatcher (so render-node-probe and
  libdrm identify the node), refuses KMS numbers (< DRM_COMMAND_BASE),
  and forwards everything else to kbase_ioctl() on the same file.
  kbase reads _IOC_SIZE(cmd), copies that many bytes, dispatches on the
  in-buffer function id, copies back -- the UK ABI passes through
  untouched, including the 32-byte r7p0 submit shape (translated to
  stride-64 by the compat header before re-issue; the shim itself only
  carries native 10.6 calls the winsys already emits).
- mmap routing: GEM fake offsets live above DRM_FILE_PAGE_OFFSET_START
  (32-bit 0x100000+), kbase cookies below BASE_MEM_COOKIE_BASE + small
  (0x40000 + n*0x1000), so the ranges cannot collide; this driver
  creates no GEM objects, hence no drm_mmap_locked lookup can succeed
  on this node anyway.  Forward unconditionally with the kctx installed.
- No modeset: driver_features has NO DRIVER_MODESET, so no control or
  legacy minors are allocated and no CRTC/encoder/connector exists.
  KMS ioctls are not in the table and the fops wrapper refuses their
  numbers, so SETCRTC/PAGE_FLIP on this node fail loudly instead of
  reaching display hardware.
- Coexistence: binds a NEW platform device created in-code
  (platform_device_register_simple("drm-kbase-shim", -1)), matches NO DT
  compatible, steals no DECON/DSIM registers, takes no clocks or power
  domains, installs no IRQ.  The vendor fbdev (DECON/DSIM) and the M1
  exynos card0/controlD64 path keep their devices; /dev/fb0 + fbdev X
  are unaffected.  vblank/IRQ hooks are NULL.

Kconfig/Makefile wiring (integrator applies; mirrors the backport
series wiring patch shape):
- drivers/gpu/drm/Kconfig: `source "drivers/gpu/drm/kbase-shim/Kconfig"`
  (one line, inside the existing DRM menu, no symbol changes).
- drivers/gpu/drm/Makefile: `obj-$(CONFIG_DRM_KBASE_SHIM) += kbase-shim/`
  (one line; empty when the symbol is off).
- drivers/gpu/drm/kbase-shim/Kconfig: tristate DRM_KBASE_SHIM,
  `depends on DRM && DRM_PLATFORM && MALI_MIDGARD`, default n.
- drivers/gpu/drm/kbase-shim/Makefile: `obj-$(CONFIG_DRM_KBASE_SHIM) +=
  kbase_shim.o` (single module object).

Verification (host-side; no build/flash in this slice per slice rules):
- `patch -p1 --dry-run` clean on scratch copies (integrator re-runs
  `git apply --check` at build time).  Context verified byte-exact
  against the 3.10.108 tree (drm_drv.c ioctl table :63-168 + dispatcher
  :373-480, drm_fops.c drm_stub_open/drm_open_helper/drm_release
  :180-560, drm_stub.c drm_minor_get_id/drm_get_minor/drm_put_minor
  :110-128/:328-424, drm_platform.c :42-110, exynos_drm_drv.c
  fops/driver struct :240-300, kbase kbase_open/release/ioctl/mmap +
  create/destroy/find_device :1203-1420/:2126 + context.c:38-199).
- `gcc -fsyntax-only` clean against the stub headers in this slice
  (host DRM/kbase header stand-ins; the patch itself is reviewed by
  inspection against the tree excerpts above, not compiled here --
  only the Integrator compiles it in-tree).
- On-device (M1Verify-owned, post-M1-flash): `ls /dev/dri` shows
  card0 controlD64 renderD128; `render-node-probe /dev/dri/renderD128`
  reports H2 (kbase handshake answers, panfrost GET_PARAM absent);
  winsys `kbw-selftest` full path passes against the render node;
  `SETCRTC` from the render fd returns -EINVAL (never touches display).

Companion host proof: code/gpu31/shim/stride_test (make check: native +
qemu-aarch64 PASS) proves the fixed r7p0 header emits stride-64 batches
the kernel's jd_submit accepts.
